[{ "action": "append", "id": "event-modals", "value" : "<div tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"modal-6af6b236-7445-40ef-b5fc-34c8a7929e9b\" class=\"modal\"><div class=\"modal-dialog modal-lg\"><div class=\"modal-content\"><div class=\"modal-header\"><a data-dismiss=\"modal\" class=\"close\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-remove\"></span></a><p class=\"text-muted\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-road\"></span> Research Papers</p><strong>Wed 19 Nov 2025 11:50 - 12:00 at <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/room/ase-2025/ase-2025-venue-grand-hall-5\" class=\"room-link navigate\">Grand Hall 5</a></strong> - <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/track/ase-2025/ase-2025-papers#program\" class=\"navigate\">Security 4</a> <small>Chair(s): <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/saeidtizpazniari\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\">Saeid Tizpaz-Niari</a></small></div><div class=\"modal-body\"><div class=\"bg-primary event-title\"><h4><span data-event-star=\"6af6b236-7445-40ef-b5fc-34c8a7929e9b\" title=\"Add event to your program\"><span class=\"text-disabled glyphicon glyphicon-star-empty\"></span></span><strong>Uncovering Prompt Elements: Cloning System Prompts from Behavioral Traces</strong></h4></div><div class=\"bg-info event-description\"><p>We introduce prompt cloning, a new class of black-box attacks that reconstruct system prompt functionality without accessing or extracting its actual text. Unlike traditional prompt leakage, prompt cloning aims to replicate the behavioral effect of the original prompt. Our core insight is that, even under strong alignment training and prompt-level defenses, system prompts leave observable behavioral traces in model outputs. We show that these behaviors can be decomposed into key semantic elements and selectively exposed using harmless, element-sensitive queries. By aggregating these signals, we reconstruct behaviorally equivalent prompts that closely mimic the original. Experiments across real-world LLM applications\u2014under the strongest available prompt defenses\u2014demonstrate that our cloned prompts achieve up to 85% semantic similarity, improving over base LLMs by up to 8%. These results reveal a critical weakness in current prompt protection practices: hiding prompt text is not enough. Our findings call for rethinking prompt security in behavior-aware terms.</p>\n<p></p><p></p><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/yiqian\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Yi Qian<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University</span></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/pengfei\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Pengfei <span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University</span></h5></div></div></a></div></div><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/haowu4\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Hao Wu\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/haowu4/65633561-3fe3-440c-a0f9-7a0d51df7135/micro-avatar?1783971162000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Hao Wu<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\"></span></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/ligengchen\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Ligeng Chen<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Honor Device Co., Ltd</span></h5></div></div></a></div></div><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2025/bingmao\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Bing Mao<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Nanjing University</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>China</small></h5></div></div></a></div></div></div><br></div><div class=\"modal-footer\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/details/ase-2025/ase-2025-papers/132/Uncovering-Prompt-Elements-Cloning-System-Prompts-from-Behavioral-Traces\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"btn btn-default navigate\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-new-window\"></span> All Details</a></span><a href=\"#\" data-dismiss=\"modal\" class=\"btn btn-default\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-remove\"></span> Close</a></div></div></div></div>"},{ "action": "runscript", "value